.TH SPT-KILL 1  "kill " 
.SH NAME
kill \- Terminate every running `spt` instance on this host
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBkill\fR [\fB\-\-force\fR] [\fB\-\-include\-self\fR] [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fB\-\-name\fR] [\fB\-\-timeout\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Terminate every running `spt` instance on this host
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-force\fR
Skip the graceful signal and go straight to a hard kill (`SIGKILL` / `TerminateProcess`). Default: send a graceful signal (`SIGTERM` / `TerminateProcess` with grace window) first
.TP
\fB\-\-include\-self\fR
Include the current process in the kill list (the calling `spt` itself). Off by default — typical use is "kill all the other ones."
.TP
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Print what would be killed without actually signalling anything
.TP
\fB\-\-name\fR \fI<NAME>\fR
Override the basename matched against running processes. Plain substring match; case\-insensitive. Defaults to `spt` (Unix) / `spt.exe` (Windows)
.TP
\fB\-\-timeout\fR \fI<DURATION>\fR [default: 5s]
Per\-process grace window before the platform terminate returns. Defaults to 5 seconds. Honoured on Windows (`WaitForSingleObject`); informational on Unix where `SIGTERM` is asynchronous
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help
